-An electric field : an invisible area around charged particles defined by force, direction, and strength. 
It tells us how a positive charge will push or pull other objects nearby

Direction: Points outward away from positive charges and inward toward negative charges.
Strength formula: \(E = \frac{F}{q}\) (Force divided by the test charge).

-Since at every point the electric field is a vector, it is called a vector field > Magnitude and Direction

-The field of a point charge

For an isolated positive point charge the force on a positive test charge is directed away from the centre, 
and so the field is also directed away from the centre.

On the other hand for an isolated negative point charge the force on the test charge is in the opposite direction, 
and so the field is directed towards the centre.

-An electric field vector is a vector field 
representing §the electrostatic force§ experienced per unit charge, 
pointing outward from positive charges and inward toward negative charges, 
measured in volts per meter or newtons per coulomb

Electrostatic force, also called Coulomb’s force, arises between charged particles due to their electric charges. It is a fundamental interaction in nature and is part of the electromagnetic force, one of the four fundamental forces in the universe. The force can be attractive if the charges are opposite, 
or repulsive if the charges are the same (both positive or both negative) and acts along the line joining the charges



